# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# EOL_01 — LINE ENDING POLICY. THIS FILE DECIDES, NOT EACH DEVELOPER'S core.autocrlf.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: until this block, the repository had no text policy, so every clone's line
# endings were whatever that machine's core.autocrlf happened to be. The repo accumulated a MIX of
# CRLF-stored and LF-stored blobs, and the first `git add .` from a machine with autocrlf=true
# normalised 91 previously-CRLF files in one go — 24,143 lines of pure whitespace churn landing in
# the same commit as real work, burying a 17-file change inside a 108-file diff.
#
# `text=auto` moves the decision into the repository: every text file is stored LF, and each
# working copy gets the endings its platform wants on checkout. Nobody's local setting can move a
# file's stored form again, so this class of churn cannot recur.
#
# ⚠️ WINDOWS SCRIPTS ARE PINNED TO CRLF IN THE WORKING TREE. cmd.exe parses .bat/.cmd byte by byte,
#    and a label or `goto` in an LF-only batch file can be misread or silently skipped. dev.cmd and
#    Build.cmd are the only supported ways to build this product (05_SYSTEM_LIFECYCLE_STORAGE.md
#    §4a SYS-DEVBUILD), so a clone on a machine with autocrlf=false must NOT receive them LF-only.
#    `eol=crlf` guarantees CRLF on checkout everywhere, independent of any user setting.
#
# ⚠️ AFTER CHANGING THIS FILE, RUN `git add --renormalize .` ONCE and commit the result ALONE.
#    A renormalisation touches hundreds of files; mixed into a feature commit it makes that commit
#    unreviewable, which is exactly the failure this policy exists to prevent.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Default: git decides what is text, stores it LF, checks it out native.
* text=auto

# Source and markup — text, normalised.
*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.axaml     text
*.xaml      text
*.csproj    text
*.sln       text eol=crlf
*.props     text
*.targets   text
*.json      text
*.md        text
*.txt       text
*.yml       text
*.yaml      text
*.manifest  text
*.resx      text
*.config    text

# Windows scripts — MUST arrive CRLF in the working tree. See the warning above.
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Unix scripts — MUST arrive LF, even on Windows.
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Binary via Git LFS — never touched by the text filters (`-text` below already says so).
*.dll filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.exe filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.com filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.zip filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mp3 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mp4 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mkv filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.avi filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mov filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.7z filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.pdb filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
